d law is  a pretty basic one and there's been a  lot of people get in trouble recently by  not abiding by this law this isn't just  speaking in Australia this is speaking  worldwide a couple months ago somebody  shut down Heathrow Airport because they  flew their drone to  over the airport Gaza which is the Civil  Aviation Safety Authority in Australia  regulates all our drone laws and they've  recently updated their can I fly their  drone app to the open skies app if you  download the open sky app for both  Android and iPhone I'm gonna link it  down below with a couple of other things  to help you guys out here you can  actually find out where you're legally  allowed to fly what kind of limitations  there are and all depends if you're  flying recreationally or for commercial  use  so the can I fly their app is the old  version from cars though that's been  replaced by open sky and honestly can I  fly there was a half-decent  app but it was very buggy and glitchy  from what I found from the open sky app  it's a lot more user friendly and so  fast so going from a bugs point of view  however the basic rule of thumb is if  you're 5.5 k's anywhere in the radius of  an airport make sure you do not fly a  drone and if you're unsure open the open  sky app take a look and find out if you  can safely launch your drone the fourth  law is a basic one and also kind of  tricky to do in a sense so the 4th  Australian law states that you can only  fly one drone at any given time I don't  really understand how you're going to be  operating two drones at the same time  that seems a bit counterproductive and  well dangerous but yeah make sure you're  only flying one drone at a time and the  fifth law is super simple make sure you  respect everybody's privacy we don't  want anybody find their drones next to  somebody's window looking into things  and causing havoc around the street so  if your neighbors have some concerns  make sure you have a chat to them first  tell them that you're not gonna be  flying anywhere near their house or if  you're flying commercially make sure you  give everybody a proper warning before  you go and do your commercial shoot okay  so let's say you've been flying your  drone for a while now and you've  mastered the art of drone flying and  maybe you thinking to yourself that  drone cost me a lot of money I'd love to  make it back somehow  well if you're looking to become a  commercial pilot or offer your services  in a commercial industry you actually  need to be registered with Kaiser in  Australia there are a couple of things  to keep in mind here and one of the best  things to know  is the fact that if your drone is under  two kilos you actually fall into an  exemption category which makes becoming  a commercial pilot so much easier but  let's have a quick thing here if you  have a drone and you have a service to  offer somebody what's a quick way you're  able to start a business and actually  talk to somebody and start getting some  drone footage out there in the real  world  well in my experience the best way to  offer your services as a drone pilot in  any small town or any area that's  lacking these abilities is to simply go  out and knock on real estate agents  doors real estate agents love making  their ads stand out and drone photos and  drone videos make sure they pop so if  you're able to come in at a very  competitive price and make sure you're  giving extremely high-quality service  well real estate agents are going to  love you and you're going to be staying  up all night editing photos getting  those drone pictures out the door and  you're gonna have your first ever drone  business but before we get to any of  that you really have to make sure you  abide by the cars or safety laws and  register as an official drone pilot on  the cars or website so as I mentioned if  your drone falls under the 2 kilo weight  limit you actually fall in an exemption  category so this means you don't have to  go through all the cars of processes but  get your remote pilot's license get your  remote operational certificate and do  these very expensive and very long  training courses one of the great things  about owning a maverick air or a  maverick Pro maverick zoom is the fact  they all fall under the sub 2 kilo  category but basically to become a  commercially registered pilot with cars  are in the exclusion category all we  have to do is go online and fill out a  couple forms and make sure you inform  Kaza  that you're going to fly in a commercial  space from here on in however to get a  drone pilot's license in Australia with  a drone that weighs more than 2 kilos  you're gonna have to go to a training  course near your local city this  training course actually comprised of  two components and it's the remote  pilots license and the remote operator  certificate I think from memory takes  about three days to get both of these  and it is a little bit costly but again  it is well worth it if you  during that commercial space as I've  mentioned before there are a couple of  new drone laws coming to effect in  potentially early 2020 so Caza has sent  out a mass email to everybody on its  drone emailing list stating a couple of  the different facts and a couple of the  new laws that may potentially come in  early 2020 there are a few things that I  personally think are great for the drone  community and a few things that are  going to really discourage people from  owning a drone and making drones seem  even worse than they are in the public  eye today so regardless if you're  planning on using a drone for  recreational fun purposes or if you're  planning on using it as a commercial  drone you're gonna have to register it  with the Casa Association so when you  think about it it's great that everybody  has to be registered but having  everybody registered means somebody in  the background has to set all this up  which means it's going to cost you money  to have a registration every single year  at the moment if you're thinking of  owning a recreational drone and flying  it whenever once twice a million times a  year it's potentially going to cost you  about $30 a year just to have this drone  registration on the flip side if you're  thinking about becoming a commercially  registered pilot along with all those  remote pilot license and remote operator  certificate fees you're also gonna have  to pay a yearly fee to keep your remote  pilot license and your drone registered  at the moment from what I've been told  it's about two hundred and fifty dollars  really just to keep your license the  second proposed change is something  that's well it should be pretty obvious  but I guess as a kids grow up you feel  they're more and more like adults and  you give them a lot more responsibility  and you stop watching over them  eventually however now they're proposing  that anybody under the age of 16 is  going to have to be supervised by an  adult when flying a drone now this is  great for somebody who's irresponsible  and maybe risking other people's lives  by flying a drone over into an airport  however it also is discouraging at  younger kids and younger photographers  videographers cinematographers from  reaching into this industry and really  honing their skills in before they go  out into the real world and start  exploring these potential opportunities  so in my opinion I'm not really too sure  if this is a good thing or a bad thing  but I think the kids that are really  passionate about photography and  videography especially drone photography  they're gonna be happy with their  parents or any adult standing next to  them at all times as they fly the drone  and they're still gonna be just as  passionate and love it just as much  thanks so much for watching guys that's  all I have for you today if you enjoyed  the video make sure you smash that  subscribe button maybe drop a comment  let me know what you thought and as  always I'll see you next Monday

